1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,400
So this summer at Scout Camp, what a wonderful time we had.

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,700
I was talking with the aquatics director and you know we were just talking, and he said,

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:19,900
yeah, he was a little aggravated because he had to stop one of the scouts from peeing

5
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,800
in the pool that morning.

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,600
I know it's kind of unpleasant, but you know it does happen.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,300
And I said: you know how do you do that?

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:33,000
I mean, I said you know, I guess probably a lot of boys end up doing that,

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:33,500
Don't they?

10
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:36,400
He says yeah, but not when they're standing on the diving board.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,400
Hey, this is podcast number 90..

12
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:12,400
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast.

13
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:13,600
This is Clark Green.

14
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,900
Hey, let's take a look and see what came through the wires this week.

15
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:25,300
First of all, Anne Voyageur. Anne Voyageur left a comment over on iTunes

16
00:01:25,899 --> 00:01:29,600
Says about the podcast: a wealth of knowledge on all matters of scouting.

17
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:33,500
I've been inspired, learned, laughed and enjoyed every minute of these podcasts.

18
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:34,700
Please keep them coming.

19
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:35,800
They're great.

20
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,600
Well, thank you, Anne Voyageur.

21
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:41,200
I do appreciate the vote of confidence there.

22
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:48,000
I also heard from Tom Brewer and he is up in Ottawa, Ontario, Canada,

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,900
where he's a beaver scout leader in the third Orleans beaver colony

24
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:55,800
and the third Orleans scout group

25
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:03,400
And he just got in touch to tell me the story that you heard just before during the opening.

26
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:06,400
So if you liked it, it goes to Tom's credit.

27
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,400
If you didn't, you know I'll get in touch with Tom and tell him,

28
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:19,100
And he wrote to thank me for the podcast and says that he always enjoys them.

29
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:24,500
So this morning I'm putting together things for the podcast, getting ready to do my recording,

30
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:29,100
And I get an email question and I think the email question that I got is important enough

31
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,400
that we need to spend some real time talking about it,

32
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:33,900
So it's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

33
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:40,700
I'm working on a couple of interviews and some more information about the new guide to advancement

34
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:46,799
and the new Eagle Project workbook, because I think these also are tremendously important things.

35
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:53,800
I had a couple of posts about realistic expectations for scouts and some things that are new in the

36
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:59,700
Advancement Guide, and we're going to spend more time talking about those. but we're going

37
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:04,200
to use this podcast to address this email question first and that'll be coming up in

38
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:05,200
just a second.

39
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,000
As a matter of fact, it takes up the remainder of the podcast.

40
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,500
So let's get started, shall we?

41
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:46,200
Hi, I'm Kevin Callan and I'm listening to

42
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:59,600
the Scout Master podcast.

43
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,600
Beautiful, beautiful.

44
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,600
Write me a letter, send it my name

45
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:09,600
Email. that is folks.

46
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,600
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

47
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:24,600
This week I heard from John Sloan via email.

48
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:28,600
John's a scout master and he had this question to ask.

49
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,600
He says my troop had a camp out this past weekend.

50
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:35,600
I had adults camp where we could hear the scouts if they were loud, but could not

51
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,600
see anything other than lantern and firelight.

52
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:43,600
I loved the idea of us being separated from them to let them do their own thing, and it

53
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,600
worked out very well.

54
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,600
The boys loved it and they had a great time.

55
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:51,600
They ended up way behind schedule, but they achieved most of what they set out to do.

56
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:54,600
The setup was quite different from what they've been used to.

57
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:59,600
I've only been scout master since April and this was the first time I was really able

58
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,600
to get us adults completely away from them.

59
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:06,600
My father is the troop committee chairman and thinks I need to be in camp with the boys

60
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:07,600
at all times.

61
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:11,600
He's supportive of my push for a true boy led troop, but he doesn't really trust them

62
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,600
to be on their own on camp outs.

63
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:18,600
When I was discussing this with my wife. she suggested that my dad did have a point.

64
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:22,600
How am I supposed to ensure their safety if I'm not there with them?

65
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:26,600
She is concerned because something very bad can happen very quickly.

66
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:28,600
She's right, it can.

67
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:30,600
I didn't have a great answer for her.

68
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:34,600
All I really have is that I trust them to act like scouts and be safe.

69
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:38,600
Accidents can happen whether I'm watching them happen or not,

70
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:43,600
But I'm not sure that any of that would fly with a parent when their son is in

71
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,600
the hospital getting stitches. and they found out I was drinking coffee

72
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:49,600
at another campsite when the accident occurred.

73
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:53,600
So, long story short, how do you ensure that boys are safe on a camp out

74
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,600
when you're not in their campsite with them?

75
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:02,600
John, that is a fantastic question and I really appreciate you getting in touch

76
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,600
and I hope that I'll be able to answer it thoroughly for you.

77
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:07,600
How do you do what I suggest,

78
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:12,600
And that is to let the boys camp from a distance from the adults

79
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:17,600
so that they can do their thing and maintain safety.

80
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:22,600
So let's discuss exactly how this should happen.

81
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:27,600
Well, John, I've got to tell you your father is right and your wife is right.

82
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,600
They're smart people, but you already knew that,

83
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:32,600
And you are right.

84
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:40,600
Everything that you wrote is perfectly valid and we need to reconcile them all together.

85
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:46,600
The short answer is: we understand and assess the risks of the particular activity

86
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,600
that we are undertaking

87
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:52,600
And then we take all prudent measures to mitigate those risks.

88
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:57,600
We're never going to eliminate them, but we're going to take every prudent measure

89
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:59,600
that we can to mitigate them.

90
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:06,600
You know, scouts are boys and boys are infamously bad assessors of risk.

91
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:12,600
They can be careless and inattentive and naive and they may disregard safety measures.

92
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:16,600
The ever-present question is: how do we manage this risk appropriately

93
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,600
while we still allow scouts the latitude to be scouts?

94
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,600
The BSA has given us some excellent tools to do this,

95
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:31,600
And the best one for the purposes of this discussion is the Suite 16 of BSA Safety.

96
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,600
If you are unfamiliar with that, you need to get familiar with it,

97
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,600
And I will have a link to the Suite 16 of BSA Safety

98
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:44,600
in the post that contains this podcast at scoutmastercgcom.

99
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:49,600
If we follow the procedures and policies in the Suite 16,

100
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:55,600
we're going to mitigate or limit risks our scouts are exposed to.

101
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,600
Now we're not going to eliminate them, but we are going to mitigate them

102
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,600
and we're going to keep them safe.

103
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,600
Now I'm not going to try and go through all 16 points here,

104
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:09,600
but I'm going to point out a couple that help to answer John's question.

105
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:14,600
The first point of the Suite 16 is qualified supervision,

106
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:18,600
and I'm going to read that exactly as it appears online:

107
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:22,600
Every BSA activity should be supervised by a conscientious adult

108
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:27,600
who understands and knowingly accepts responsibility for the well-being

109
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:32,600
and safety of the children and youth in his or her care.

110
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:37,600
The supervisor should be sufficiently trained, experienced and skilled

111
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:41,600
in the activity to be confident of his or her ability to lead

112
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:46,600
and teach the necessary skills to respond effectively

113
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:48,600
in the event of an emergency

114
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:52,600
Field. knowledge of all applicable BSA standards

115
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:56,600
and a commitment to implement and follow BSA policy and procedures

116
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:01,600
are essential parts of the supervisor's qualification.

117
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:04,600
So let's parse that a little bit.

118
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:09,600
What qualifies any scout leader to supervise an average camp out?

119
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:14,600
Well, first, he or she understands the risk and accepts the responsibilities.

120
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:19,600
They're also familiar with and able to teach the necessary skills

121
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,600
and they're prepared to respond to emergencies.

122
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:26,600
They must be sufficiently trained, experienced and skilled.

123
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:31,600
Now there are three tests there for qualifying a person

124
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:33,600
to supervise a scouting activity.

125
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:38,600
They have to be trained, number one, experienced, number two

126
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:40,600
and skilled, number three.

127
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:45,600
You can't just be trained, you can't just be experienced,

128
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:48,600
or you can't just be skilled. You need to be all three.

129
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,600
Now, training: that's a fairly simple thing

130
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:58,600
and the BSA has provided wonderful resources for safety training.

131
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:01,600
If you haven't already, you need to make sure

132
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:03,600
that you take all of the safety training

133
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,600
that you can possibly take.

134
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,600
Most of those courses are online.

135
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:11,600
There's lightning safety or weather wise.

136
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:17,600
there's safety afloat. there's safe swim defense

137
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:19,600
and whether or not you think you're going on a swimming

138
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:23,600
or a float trip or you're going to be exposed

139
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:26,600
to harsh weather conditions or not, take all of them,

140
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:29,600
because it will start to get in your mind

141
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:35,600
the BSA approach to safety and it is extraordinarily important

142
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:39,600
and I hope that everyone takes advantage

143
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,600
of all of the safety training they can possibly get.

144
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:46,600
You have to match your experience and skill

145
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:49,600
to the activity and to the participants.

146
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:52,600
Let's take safe swim defense.

147
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:56,600
You're going to go someplace. there's a lake

148
00:10:56,600 --> 00:10:59,600
and part of what you're planning on doing that weekend

149
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,600
is boys are going to go swimming.

150
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:04,600
First of all, have you been trained in safe swim defense?

151
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,600
Have you actually supervised scouts

152
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,600
when they've been swimming?

153
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,600
Do you have any experience and skill?

154
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:12,600
Your experience and skill is going to determine

155
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:14,600
how you apply your training.

156
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:16,600
The first few times you supervise scout swimming

157
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:18,600
using the safe swim defense,

158
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:20,600
you're going to be extraordinarily conservative

159
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:22,600
in your approach and as you gain skill and experience

160
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:24,600
you'll become more comfortable-

161
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:27,600
but never inattentive or cavalier-

162
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:30,600
in applying the training that you've been giving.

163
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:34,600
But you get more experience and you gain more skill.

164
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:37,600
When a new scout master goes camping,

165
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:39,600
they're going to supervise at a distance

166
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,600
that matches their training, ability and skill.

167
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:46,600
A brand new scout leader is going to spend more time

168
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:50,600
in a scout's campsite, observing without interfering,

169
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:52,600
until their experience and skill

170
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,600
permits them to, you know,

171
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:56,600
broaden the distance a little bit.

172
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:59,600
So what risks are involved in an average camping trip?

173
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:01,600
Well, we think of things like fire

174
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:03,600
and fire heats, water,

175
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:06,600
so hot liquids, you know, those moving around a campsite

176
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:08,600
that can be kind of dangerous

177
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:11,600
Edge tools like knives and hatchets and saws.

178
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:14,600
these are all pretty obvious risks.

179
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:17,600
Now, do we understand the risks?

180
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:20,600
Have we taught or overseen the teaching of the skills

181
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:22,600
to handle those risks safely?

182
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:24,600
What kind of experience do we have with them?

183
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:27,600
Do we review them constantly with our youth leaders

184
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:30,600
and our adult leaders?

185
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:32,600
Let's look at a very specific risk.

186
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:35,600
Let's look at knives or edge tools

187
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,600
like hatchets and saws?

188
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:41,600
Has every scout completed totenship training

189
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:44,600
before they start using those things in camp?

190
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:48,600
Are the safety practices taught by the totenship course

191
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:51,600
maintained by youth and adult leaders?

192
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,600
The totenship course doesn't eliminate risk.

193
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:57,600
I mean, one of the trips I took to the emergency room

194
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:01,600
was for a scout who managed to cut himself

195
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:03,600
to the extent where he needed eight stitches.

196
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:05,600
He did this with a pocket knife

197
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:08,600
and it happened about 20 minutes

198
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:11,600
after he had gotten his totenship card

199
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:14,600
And he was a few feet up from me at the time.

200
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:17,600
The only way I was going to prevent that accident

201
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:19,600
was to stand over the scout

202
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:22,600
and then grab the knife just before it sliced his thumb.

203
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:25,600
I'm not gifted with superhuman foresight,

204
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:28,600
so I ended up at the emergency room.

205
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:31,600
Now, was I adequately supervising the scout?

206
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:33,600
I would say yes.

207
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:36,600
Did he have the skills and training

208
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:39,600
to be able to prevent that accident?

209
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:41,600
Well, he had the training,

210
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:43,600
but he hadn't developed the experience or skills yet.

211
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:46,600
How can I be adequately supervising scouts

212
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:48,600
if they're out of sight?

213
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:50,600
Well, this guy was right there,

214
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:54,600
A couple feet away, Zingo cuts his thumb.

215
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:57,600
What I had done was I had done my best

216
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:00,600
to mitigate the risk and I used common sense.

217
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:03,600
So he was trained in totenship skills.

218
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:06,600
He had demonstrated that he knew them.

219
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:09,600
but you know he's a boy, he's inexperienced

220
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:12,600
and there's a cut.

221
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:14,600
Now, that's one out of hundreds of boys,

222
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:16,600
at times going camping.

223
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:19,600
So, as John mentioned in his e-mail,

224
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:22,600
you know injuries can happen.

225
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:25,600
accidents can happen when I'm standing right there.

226
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:28,600
So what's the difference between them happening right there

227
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:32,600
and those happening if the scouts are camped a distance from me?

228
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:34,600
Well, there really is no difference,

229
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:37,600
because what we're looking for is the proper application

230
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:40,600
of your training, experience and skills.

231
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:43,600
Now, in that case that I just described,

232
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:45,600
I applied my training experience and skills,

233
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:48,600
still had an accident. you know,

234
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:51,600
That's the way the ball bounces sometimes.

235
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:54,600
So let's talk about how do we apply training,

236
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,600
experience and skills supervising our scouts

237
00:14:57,600 --> 00:14:59,600
if they're camped at some remove

238
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:01,600
from where the adults are camping.

239
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:04,600
Well, I ask a lot of questions

240
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:07,600
And by asking questions I assess risk

241
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:09,600
and I apply common sense.

242
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:11,600
I ask a patrol leader or senior patroller

243
00:15:11,600 --> 00:15:13,600
what they will be doing.

244
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:15,600
If there's anything that sounds remotely

245
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:17,600
like it's a safety issue,

246
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:20,600
I'm going to ask them questions about mitigating risk

247
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:23,600
for that particular activity.

248
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:24,600
If I ask the senior patroller, I say:

249
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:25,600
what are you guys going to be doing.

250
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,600
Well, we're going to be gathering firewood,

251
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:29,600
Say, okay, you're going to be cutting the firewood,

252
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:30,600
Yeah,

253
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:31,600
What are you going to be using?

254
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:32,600
An axe?

255
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:34,600
Okay, so tell me about your axe yard.

256
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:36,600
Where have you set that up?

257
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:39,600
Tell me a couple of safety rules about hand axes.

258
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:41,600
If this is a new senior patrol leader

259
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,600
or a lot of inexperienced boys,

260
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:45,600
I might say: look, since you're doing this

261
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:47,600
for the first time, make sure to come and get me

262
00:15:47,600 --> 00:15:49,600
before you start swinging any axes or hatchets

263
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:51,600
so I can look at and see what's happening.

264
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:53,600
When it's time to cook dinner,

265
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:55,600
I may wander through patrol campsite

266
00:15:55,600 --> 00:15:57,600
or observe closely

267
00:15:57,600 --> 00:15:59,600
and make sure that the campfire is okay,

268
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:02,600
make sure that the cooking setup is okay

269
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:04,600
and if I see any problems

270
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:07,600
or not, if I see problems or not,

271
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:09,600
I'm still going to ask questions.

272
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:12,600
What's going to happen if that pot of boiling water tips over?

273
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:15,600
Is there a better place you can set up your camp stove?

274
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:18,600
Is there a better setup for your campfire?

275
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:21,600
If it looks eminently dangerous, I'm going to stop it.

276
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:23,600
I'm going to stop it and I'm going to say:

277
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:26,600
look, guys, you're in a little bit of danger here.

278
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:29,600
so let me make some suggestions about how you can fix that.

279
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,600
You need to change the campfire setup this way.

280
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:34,600
You need to put the stove on a better ground.

281
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:37,600
You need to make sure that you have pot holders

282
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:40,600
before you mess around with that pot of hot water,

283
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:42,600
Just that type of thing.

284
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:44,600
I can do this without interfering,

285
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:46,600
without doing everything for them.

286
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:50,600
I assess risk and I supervise

287
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:53,600
that particular activity by asking questions.

288
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:56,600
If I'm sitting with my cup of coffee

289
00:16:56,600 --> 00:16:59,600
in a remote campsite from the boys

290
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:01,600
and I hear some shouting,

291
00:17:01,600 --> 00:17:03,600
what am I going to do?

292
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:05,599
I'm going to see what's going on.

293
00:17:05,599 --> 00:17:08,599
If scouts are going to take off on a hike,

294
00:17:08,599 --> 00:17:10,599
I want to know where they're going.

295
00:17:10,599 --> 00:17:12,599
I want to know if they have a cell phone.

296
00:17:12,599 --> 00:17:14,599
I want to know if they have a first aid kit.

297
00:17:14,599 --> 00:17:16,599
I want to know if there's a signal for the cell phone.

298
00:17:16,599 --> 00:17:18,599
I want to know what time they will return.

299
00:17:18,599 --> 00:17:21,599
I want to look at the group of scouts that's going

300
00:17:21,599 --> 00:17:24,599
and I want to assess whether or not

301
00:17:24,599 --> 00:17:27,599
they're going to need a couple of older scouts along with them

302
00:17:27,599 --> 00:17:30,600
or if they're going to need a couple of adults along with them.

303
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:32,600
If there's a shadow of a doubt,

304
00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:34,600
I am going to do whatever I can

305
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,600
to apply my training, skills and experience

306
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:40,600
to mitigate whatever risks are present.

307
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:42,600
If we're camped near a body of water,

308
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:44,600
I'm going to ask my senior patrol leader

309
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:46,600
what the rules for swimming are.

310
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:49,600
I'm going to ask him if scouts have been reminded of them.

311
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:52,600
I'm going to ask him if he thinks they can go swimming or not.

312
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:55,600
You know- and they can't- unless an adult, trained

313
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:58,600
and safe twit swim defense is present.

314
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:00,600
I'm asking him if taking off your shoes

315
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:02,600
and raiding in the creek is a good idea,

316
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:03,600
Why or why not?

317
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:05,600
I ask about the buddy system.

318
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:08,600
I ask how they're managing the buddy system.

319
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:11,600
I ask a lot of questions, lots and lots of questions.

320
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:13,600
So what happens when a couple of scouts

321
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:16,600
sneak off and have a hatchet throwing contest?

322
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:19,600
What happens when somebody decides to see

323
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:23,600
what it's like if they throw a lighter into the fire?

324
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:25,600
Can I control or supervise scouts

325
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:27,600
who are willingly and knowingly

326
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:29,600
undertaking something in the contravention

327
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,600
of established safety standards?

328
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:33,600
Well, no, I can't.

329
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:35,600
And that's when I need to consider

330
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:37,600
0.8 of the Suite 16..

331
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:40,600
And 0.8 is skill level limits.

332
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,600
And I'm going to read exactly what that says:

333
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:45,600
There is a minimum skill level requirement

334
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:47,600
for every activity

335
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:50,600
And the supervisor must identify

336
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:52,600
and recognize this minimum skill level

337
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:54,600
and be sure that none are put at risk

338
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:58,600
by attempting activity beyond their ability.

339
00:18:58,600 --> 00:19:01,600
So I have to know my scout's abilities.

340
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:04,600
Do I know my youth leaders well?

341
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:07,600
Do I know what their strengths and weaknesses are?

342
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:09,600
Have they demonstrated the ability to safely conduct

343
00:19:09,600 --> 00:19:12,600
and supervise an activity they're about to undertake?

344
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:15,600
Do I need to have adults overseeing,

345
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:18,600
instructing, observing the activity,

346
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:20,600
or can we keep our distance?

347
00:19:20,600 --> 00:19:23,600
Training experience and skill,

348
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:26,600
Asking lots and lots of questions,

349
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:28,600
Observing, without interfering?

350
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:31,600
All of these are important things.

351
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:34,600
You are going to do what you need to do

352
00:19:34,600 --> 00:19:37,600
to make sure that your scouts are safe.

353
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:40,600
You're going to apply your training

354
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:43,600
according to your experience and skill.

355
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:45,600
So it's very important to me

356
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:47,600
that you don't listen to this podcast

357
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:49,600
or read the blog and get the wrong idea.

358
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,600
You know well, Clark said

359
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:53,600
we should camp away from the boys.

360
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:55,600
So let's give that a shot.

361
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:57,600
And you know you just camp away from the boys

362
00:19:57,600 --> 00:19:59,600
and you don't apply your training, your skills

363
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:02,600
or your experience to mitigating risks.

364
00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:05,600
That would not be common sense, would it?

365
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:08,600
If you are a brand new scout leader,

366
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:11,600
you're going to be a little bit closer to the situation

367
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:13,600
until you have the experience and the skills

368
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:16,600
to be able to assess risks,

369
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:19,600
to be able to know your scouts, know their abilities

370
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:23,600
and to help them design their activities

371
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:25,600
in accordance with those things.

372
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:28,600
A prudent leader who uses their common sense

373
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,600
is not going to ever ignore safety.

374
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:33,600
They're never going to think

375
00:20:33,600 --> 00:20:35,600
well, you know, boys will be boys. what happens happens.

376
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:37,600
That's foolish.

377
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:40,600
What you're going to do is you're going to be trained,

378
00:20:40,600 --> 00:20:43,600
and that's incredibly important.

379
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:47,600
You must. you must have every aspect

380
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:50,600
of safety training that you can possibly take,

381
00:20:50,600 --> 00:20:53,600
whether you think you will ever apply it or not,

382
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:55,600
it's important.

383
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:59,600
You must review those things from time to time.

384
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:02,600
This is why most of the training, like safe swim defense

385
00:21:02,600 --> 00:21:04,600
and weather wise and trek on safely,

386
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:07,600
are only good for two years,

387
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:09,600
Because two years later you need to go back

388
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:11,600
and see if there have been any changes

389
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:13,600
or there's been any improvement on the advice

390
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:15,600
and you need to retake the training.

391
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:19,600
These are not irksome or difficult things to do.

392
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:21,600
Actually, I welcome the idea

393
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:23,600
that I need to go back and get trained

394
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:25,600
every couple of years in these things.

395
00:21:25,600 --> 00:21:27,600
I've been through safe swim defense

396
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:30,600
and safety afloat probably five or six times now,

397
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:33,600
But it's always good to renew it.

398
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:35,600
So your prudence and your common sense

399
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:37,600
are very, very important.

400
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:41,600
A sober assessment of your level of training,

401
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:43,600
your experience and your skills-

402
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,600
incredibly important.

403
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:49,600
So you adjust the way that you apply

404
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:52,600
the concept of scouts being scouts

405
00:21:52,600 --> 00:21:55,600
camping away from the adults

406
00:21:55,600 --> 00:21:58,600
to those things, to your training,

407
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:01,600
your experience and your skill level.

408
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:03,600
As you grow more comfortable and knowledgeable,

409
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:05,600
you'll be fine.

410
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:08,600
Now, in those instances where I've talked about

411
00:22:08,600 --> 00:22:10,600
a troop trip that we've taken

412
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:12,600
and I may say: well, you know,

413
00:22:12,600 --> 00:22:14,600
we were camped over here, the boys were camped over there.

414
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:16,600
Don't think for one minute

415
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:19,600
I did not have my mind

416
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:22,600
over in that scout campsite

417
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:24,600
And I was not thinking through

418
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:26,600
all of my training,

419
00:22:26,600 --> 00:22:28,600
applying my experience and skills.

420
00:22:28,600 --> 00:22:30,600
Don't think that I wasn't watching

421
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:32,600
what was going on.

422
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:34,600
Don't think that we weren't

423
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:36,600
observing and asking questions,

424
00:22:36,600 --> 00:22:38,600
because that is a constant,

425
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:41,600
constant aspect of applying the scout program

426
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:43,600
and assuring the safety of our scouts.

427
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:45,600
John, I hope that helps.

428
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:47,600
I hope that helps everyone.

